# Dagent — End-to-End Architecture (Extension → Backend → Popup)

Pin-to-pin reference of exactly how the extension currently works, from the moment a trigger fires to the moment a popup appears (or doesn't).

---

## 1. Where each piece lives

| Component | Runs where | Lifetime |
|---|---|---|
| `content-script.js` | Injected into every open tab | Re-injected fresh on every full page load/refresh |
| `classifiedCache` (the cache) | In-memory JS `Map`, inside the content script | **Lives on the user's device only**, per tab, per page load — never sent anywhere, never persisted to disk. Wiped on refresh/navigation. |
| `background.js` | Chrome's background service worker | Persists across tabs/navigation within the browser session; owns the actual network call |
| FastAPI backend | Your machine (or wherever deployed) | Stateless — holds no memory of past requests at all |

**Important:** the cache is 100% client-side. The backend never knows a cache exists — every request it receives is treated identically whether it's the extension's 1st or 500th call.

---

## 2. Scraping — what gets read off the page

**Selector:** `p, h1, h2, h3` — nothing else is scraped (no `span`, `div`, `button`, `a`, etc. at this time).

**For each matched element:**
1. Read `el.innerText || el.textContent`, trim whitespace.
2. **Discard if under 3 characters** (`MIN_TEXT_LENGTH = 3`) — filters out stray whitespace/icons-as-text.
3. **Visibility check** — must pass all of:
   - `getBoundingClientRect()` width/height > 0
   - Element's box intersects the current viewport (`0 ≤ top ≤ window.innerHeight`, etc.)
   - Computed style: not `display:none`, not `visibility:hidden`, not `opacity:0`
   - → **Only elements currently on-screen are scraped.** Scrolling past something removes it from consideration until it's back in view.
4. **Hash the text** — a fast string hash (djb2-style), producing a short base-36 key, e.g. `"k3f9x2"`. This hash is the cache key everywhere downstream.
5. **Assign a `block_id`** — a `WeakMap<Element, string>` gives each DOM element a stable id (`dagent-1`, `dagent-2`, ...) the first time it's seen, reused on subsequent scrapes of the same element.
6. **Tag captured too** — `el.tagName.toLowerCase()` (`p`, `h1`, `h2`, `h3`) is sent alongside the text, so the backend/analytics can distinguish a heading from a paragraph if ever needed. Currently the model treats all tags identically — tag is metadata, not a model input.

---

## 3. Triggers — what causes a scrape

| Trigger | Listener | Debounce | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| DOM changes | `MutationObserver` on `document.body` (childList, subtree, characterData) | **400ms** | Catches SPA content updates (React/Vue re-renders) without firing on every micro-mutation |
| Scroll | `window.scroll` (passive) | **300ms** | Fires after scrolling *stops*, not continuously while scrolling |
| Mouse idle | `window.mousemove` (passive), but debounced so it only fires after the mouse *stops* | **500ms** | Used as a general "user is still engaged" signal, not tracked continuously |

All three call the same function, `sendBlocksIfAny()`, which internally calls `scrapeVisibleBlocks()`.

---

## 4. The cache-hit / cache-miss decision (per block, per scrape cycle)

For every visible, sufficiently-long text block found in step 2:

```
hash = hash(text)

if hash in classifiedCache:
    if classifiedCache[hash] is FLAGGED
       and (now - lastShownAt[hash]) > 600ms:      # REPLAY_COOLDOWN_MS
        → replay result locally, no network call
        → update lastShownAt[hash] = now
    else:
        → do nothing (either clean-and-cached, or shown too recently)
elif hash in inFlight:
    → do nothing (already waiting on a response for this exact text)
else:
    → add to newBlocks[], mark inFlight[hash] = true
```

**Cache miss** = text never classified before → goes to the backend.
**Cache hit** = text already classified before → backend is *never* called again for that exact text, ever (for the life of that page load).

---

## 5. Response timing — cache hit vs cache miss

| | Cache miss (new text) | Cache hit (already classified) |
|---|---|---|
| Network call? | Yes — goes through background worker → FastAPI `/detect` | **No** — zero network calls |
| Batching delay | Up to 200ms (`BATCH_WINDOW_MS` in `background.js`) — waits to see if more blocks arrive before sending, so one page scan = one request, not N | None |
| Model inference time (measured) | ~120ms (spaCy sentence segmentation + Stage 1 binary + Stage 2 category on flagged sentences), logged server-side per request | None — nothing computed, just a `Map.get()` |
| Typical total time to popup | ~250–450ms from trigger to popup (batch window + inference + negligible localhost network) | **Effectively instant** — bounded only by whichever trigger's debounce caused the scrape (300–500ms), not by any processing time |
| Where the time actually goes | Backend model inference + batch window | The trigger's own debounce timer — the classification itself costs ~0ms |

**Key insight:** on a cache hit, the "slowness" people can perceive isn't computation — it's purely waiting for the *next scrape cycle* to run (i.e. waiting for you to scroll, or for the mouse-idle timer, or for a DOM mutation to fire). Once that cycle runs, the popup appears with no further delay.

---

## 6. Flagged vs clean — full behavior matrix

| Scenario | Popup shown? | Color | Content |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Cache miss**, backend says flagged | ✅ Yes | Red | Exact sentence, category, confidence % |
| **Cache miss**, backend says clean | ✅ Yes (once) | Green | "No dark pattern detected" |
| **Cache hit**, was flagged, past 600ms cooldown, still visible | ✅ Yes | Red | Same sentence/category/confidence as originally returned (replayed from memory, not re-computed) |
| **Cache hit**, was flagged, within 600ms cooldown | ❌ No | — | Suppressed to avoid double-firing from overlapping triggers |
| **Cache hit**, was clean | ❌ No, never | — | Clean cache hits are filtered out entirely by design — the green toast only ever fires on a genuinely fresh classification, never on replay |

If a single scrape cycle produces **multiple flagged detections at once** (e.g. a paragraph with 3 dark-pattern sentences), they don't stack — they **cycle one at a time**, each shown for 4 seconds (`TOAST_DISPLAY_MS`) before the next takes its place. A brand-new result (from any source — fresh or replay) always **immediately replaces** whatever's currently showing, resetting the cycle.

